WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.713
- Good morning everyone.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.630
Welcome to Yale for the closing workshop

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.020
of the Understanding the
Nature of Inference Project.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.190
I'm Priya Natarajan

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.410
an astrophysicist jointly appointed

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.170
in the departments of
astronomy and physics at Yale,

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.140
where I also serve as the current director

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.900
of Yale's flagship
interdisciplinary Franke Program

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.673
in Science and the Humanities.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.370
As you are all probably aware,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.280
this is an intellectual experiment,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.320
although David Kaiser just reminded me

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.630
the likes of him and me should not be left

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.630
to do well near any
experimental equipment,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.643
but real material equipment.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.890
So this is an experiment
in crossing boundaries,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.760
real and imagined amongst
intellectual disciplines

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.700
that are often unfortunately siloed

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.100
and do not speak to each other.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.680
And it's an experiment
in learning new languages

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.200
and metaphors as well
as new ways of thinking,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.470
probing and understanding
a really complex world.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.660
It has probably not escaped you all

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.000
that even the terms causal

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.290
and inference to say
nothing of causal inference

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.480
means something completely different

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.940
in each of our disciplines.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.000
At Yale, we decided to
take on this challenge

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.070
and explore how we could talk

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.830
and have an fruitful exchange of ideas

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.470
that disrupt tired old ways of thinking

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.130
and open up new arenas for
interdisciplinary engagement.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.080
So here we are.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.650
So our project thus far has
focused on deeply examining

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.270
the process of inference

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.330
of cause and effect across disciplines.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.830
And our motivation has been

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:54.060
to understand this process
using various kinds of evidence

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.700
and data, conceptual modeling

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.250
and simulations as proxies

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.640
for controlled scientific experiments.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.660
At this workshop,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.450
we have assembled an
extraordinary group of scholars,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.330
experts across different
intellectual fields

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.570
ranging from epidemiology,
anthropology, cosmology,

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:16.320
genetics, computer science,
mathematics to name a few,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.080
as well as philosophers of
science who study the nature

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.140
of inference and causal relationships.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.690
We look forward to
exploring the various kinds

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.420
of conceptual frameworks
used to distill the role

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.080
that modeling plays in
aiding the disentangling

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.510
of correlation and causation,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.150
which is one of the
fundamental open questions

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.385
in epistemology today,

00:02:38.385 --> 00:02:42.030
this distinction between
causation and correlation,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.680
particularly amplified in
the data of big era, AI,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.990
machine learning and so on.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.990
So the production of new
knowledge is inevitably linked

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.540
to the understanding of cause and effect

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.570
and the underlying principles,
if any exists, that is,

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.130
that map between them.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.160
And the study of causality
lies at the very core

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.260
of all scientific inquiry.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.680
Sifting out causality is also
imperative when predictions

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.410
for future behavior of complex systems

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.300
under investigation or sought.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.670
There are many approaches
to studying causation,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.940
which we will get a glimpse
of in the next two days,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.970
including probabilistic,
manipulative, counterfactual

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.290
and structural ones that
invariably comprise a set of tools,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.710
very powerful tools that
will help us discriminate

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.880
between causal connections
and statistical associations.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.360
Our multi-disciplinary
project thus far has included

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.570
a colloquium and a post
colloquium discussion series,

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.480
and this workshop is a
culmination of various teams

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.040
that we have engaged with over the course

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.230
of the last two and a half years.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.610
Our work is supported

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.060
by the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.760
at Yale, the Kempf Fund at Yale,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.160
and the John Templeton Foundation.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.660
I would like to thank
our entire core team,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.680
Dr. Tahia Kamp, the executive
director of the Franke Program

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.320
for guiding all our activities thus far

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.620
and helping us shape
this two day workshop.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.830
I wanna give a special
shout out to Larry Gladney,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.170
our Dean of Science, who
is here in the audience.

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.770
Thank you so much Larry for coming.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.110
And I want to thank all
our invited speakers,

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.750
some of whom have traveled far to be here

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.930
and for generously sharing
their ideas, being open

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.140
to sharing ideas with across
disciplinary audience.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.330
And pretty much, you know, say thank you

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.770
to everyone in the audience

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.280
for participating in this
interdisciplinary adventure.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.960
I'm really excited and look forward

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.840
to learning from everyone
over the next two days.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.570
And without further
ado, let's get started.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.230
Our first session is
themed around causation

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:53.040
and our opening speaker
is Samantha Kleinberg

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.600
and her talk is titled
Learning Causes and Using Them.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.680
Thank you Samantha.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.230
And the floor is yours.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:03.600
(audience applauding)

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.950
- Thanks so much for having me.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.243
Whoops, wrong microphone.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.720
So I started getting
interested in causality

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.600
when I was a PhD student in 2006

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.970
and I was reading a lot of biology papers,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.680
but there was something
sort of weird about them.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.970
All these papers had causality
in the title and the abstract

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.590
and then there was not really
any discussion of causality

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.160
during the paper or
causality would be, you know,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.240
coming up in the discussion

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.040
and throughout the paper
there'd be associations.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.630
And so I was sort of wondering

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.949
where did the causality happen?

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:34.890
What do people mean by causes?

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.900
Recently I went back and
found a bunch of papers

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.273
that confirmed this anecdote.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.320
84% of these studies
that one group looked at

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.270
that were just predictions in associations

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:46.803
mentioned causality somewhere.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.223
Another one looked at where
the causality happened.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.560
There's a sort of switching back and forth

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.620
between causal language
and non causal language.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.700
Another one looked at,
this is my favorite 31%

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.370
that didn't really
actually have any causality

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.280
used misleading language, but
then also in their discussion

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.570
disavowed the causality.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.540
And so they were sort
of selling the causality

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.680
in the title and the
abstract, but then saying,

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.111
actually we don't really have
any causal relationships.

00:06:13.111 --> 00:06:16.470
So it's interesting that
causality kept coming up a lot,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.660
but also there was this sort of shady,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.070
do we have causes, do we don't?

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.153
Does it matter?

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.540
At the same time, people are
also saying things like this

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.150
that actually you don't
need causes, right?

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.180
You know, and this was a nice
quote telling the best story

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.650
about the research findings.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.540
You don't need to say that X causes Y

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.270
and this is for epidemiological research.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.560
And the authors agree,

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.440
we wanna prevent disease,
we wanna intervene.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.290
Apparently causes are not
super helpful for that.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.540
And also that we actually
can't even find causes,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.640
we can't definitively claim causality.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.420
And so that seemed really bad
given all the papers talking

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.973
about causality and
claiming to find causes.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:58.350
As a computer scientist,
this came up a lot,

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.840
people saying don't worry about it, right?

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.250
If you're trying to make predictions

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.290
who caress why they work,
you only need to know

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.480
that your prediction does work.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.130
And so maybe, you know, just
give up on this pursuit.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.810
But to me it was more like this.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.420
You know, if causality isn't so important,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.370
why is everyone claiming we
have causal relationships

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.500
or trying to find causal relationships?

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.677
One might be everyone else is doing it.

00:07:21.677 --> 00:07:24.720
And so if everyone else is
mentioning causal relationships,

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.760
maybe we should too.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.480
Or there's some value in
causal relationships, right?

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.790
The same reason people lie
on dating profiles, right?

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.880
You lie about things that you
think other people care about

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:37.140
or that might make you more appealing

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.427
or make your research
more appealing, right?

00:07:38.427 --> 00:07:40.140
And I think it's kind of the second one

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.900
that there is some value
in mentioning causes

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.910
and that's why people talk about them

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.340
and maybe exaggerate their findings.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.310
So I was a computer scientist,
I guess I'm still sort

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.920
of a computer scientist at the time

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.020
because computational biology rejected me.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.600
So my PhD was computer science.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.460
And so I thought, okay, what
do computer scientists have

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.660
to say about causality?

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:01.757
And this is what that looked like.

00:08:01.757 --> 00:08:05.730
And so this was causality
in computer science in 2006

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.980
was base nets and they
were generally very simple.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.930
It was, you know, you have two variables,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.380
how do we find causal
relationships between them?

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:15.171
It wasn't super satisfying for me

00:08:15.171 --> 00:08:18.660
because I was interested in
problems that looked like this.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.870
This is an actual patient
in the neuro ICU at Columbia

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.900
where I've collaborated
with people since 2010.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.520
It's a super complex environment.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.830
There's lots of things measured.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.370
This is an advanced ICU that
actually stores all this data.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:33.600
Other ICUs don't.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.580
And so you just happen to
see things streaming by

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:36.840
in real time.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.270
Maybe a nurse writes down
some of the variables,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.520
numbers, maybe they don't.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.960
I've worked with other ICUs

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.640
where records are still kept on paper

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.263
and scanned in as PDFs.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.310
And so this is actually,
you know, cutting edge,

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.410
but it's a really complex
decision making problem.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.360
And there's this tension between this is

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.150
what reality looks like for this problem.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.540
And then we have our base
net with our two variables

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.170
and no timing or complexity
or, you know, interactions

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:05.553
between these variables.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.130
But talking to the doctors,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.161
it did seem they did want causes.

00:09:10.161 --> 00:09:12.630
They weren't going to be
satisfied with predictions

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.400
and just knowing, you know,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.790
this is what's going to
happen to the patient.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.580
I also work on decision making problems

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.140
where patients are making choices.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.750
Like in diabetes, it's not the doctor

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:26.940
helping you manage your
blood glucose every day.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.400
It's you as an individual
having to do that.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.790
And again, people don't
just want predictions.

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.040
And so I was satisfied that yes,

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.010
they actually do want
causal relationships.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:38.843
They don't wanna know.

00:09:38.843 --> 00:09:41.850
There's been a huge
history of alarms in ICUs

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:42.683
and usually that's

00:09:42.683 --> 00:09:45.660
what the computer scientists
come along and provide.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:49.320
There's alarms, lots of
bad things happen in ICU

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.270
can't do anything about a lot of them.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.210
And so the doctors,
nurses get alarm fatigue

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.650
and start ignoring the alarms.

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.290
And then what ultimately
happens is they ignore ones

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.310
that they should have listened to

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:02.193
and in bad cases patients
have died as a result,

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.800
because most of the
alarms are not actionable

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.590
and we're not actually
providing causal information.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.850
Why is this happening?

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.850
And most importantly, what can
we do about what's happening?

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.080
Nevertheless, some computer scientists

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:16.913
have actually doubled down.

00:10:16.913 --> 00:10:18.150
Not only do we not need causes,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.820
we don't actually really
need doctors either.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:25.820
So this is my favorite from
Jeff Hinton in 2016 saying,

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:28.320
you know what, just stop
training radiologist.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.560
It's just completely obvious
in five years deep learning

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.850
is gonna be able to do better,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.560
it's gonna get more experience.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.960
And then he sort of walked back and said,

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.030
well okay, if you're like a radiologist,

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.230
you're like the coyote

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:41.550
who's looked over the edge of the cliff

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:42.630
but hasn't looked down

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.880
and doesn't know there's
no ground under him.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.120
He acknowledged maybe we
need some radiologists

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.392
but we have enough already,
we don't need to train more.

00:10:51.392 --> 00:10:54.571
And so, you know, I don't
think any of us want

00:10:54.571 --> 00:10:57.015
computers giving us diagnoses,

00:10:57.015 --> 00:10:59.910
but even so this idea
that learning from data

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:01.770
and experience is all you need,

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.920
we've actually seen that's
totally wrong, right?

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.420
And so there've been lots of cases

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.050
of radiology has been a really
popular application area

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.470
for deep learning and there's
nice really quick cycle

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:17.550
of hype and then the
antidote to the hype of

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.190
as we learn deep learning
is better than radiologists.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.050
And then a week later,
actually no it's not.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:25.290
So this is an example
of one of those papers

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.630
where it's trying to predict pneumonia

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.880
and then the numbers
on the left are showing

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.793
how important these different
areas of the X-ray are.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.080
And then a sort of weird one
is in the upper left you see

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.060
there's a one next to portable

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:40.920
because the algorithm
thinks it's really important

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:42.330
that used a portable scanner

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.590
and that's gonna help me predict

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.420
whether this patient has pneumonia or not.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.600
And so it's using features
that are totally inappropriate,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.610
not useful at all, have nothing to do

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.473
with the patient's diagnosis or not.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.110
And that was a mistake that a radiologist

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.370
would absolutely never make, right?

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.440
A radiologist is not going to look

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.490
at that information at all

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.952
because they understand which
things are actually meaningful

00:12:01.952 --> 00:12:03.483
for the disease or not.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.620
Another fun case was Google Flu.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.290
So Google was using
people's search keywords

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.044
to predict cases of the flu,

00:12:12.044 --> 00:12:14.220
track cases of the flu over time.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.210
And they were doing it actually better

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:16.800
than the CDC for a while.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.270
So people got very excited.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.180
You can figure out how much
flu is in the area based on,

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:22.170
you know, searching for symptoms

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.169
and treatments and things like that.

00:12:24.169 --> 00:12:26.280
And then what happened afterwards

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.830
was suddenly Google's
predicting twice as many cases

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.530
of the flu as there actually were.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:34.920
And then now we know more
about Google Flu as a failure.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.440
My favorite thing about this
is the quote I showed you

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.514
about prediction and not
needing to know how things work

00:12:40.514 --> 00:12:43.530
was from a Google employee
after this actually happened.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:46.350
And so I don't think Google's
fully learned the lesson there

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.174
that even if you want predictions,

00:12:48.174 --> 00:12:49.770
you're not gonna have a robust prediction

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:50.910
without causal relationships.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:52.380
You're not gonna be able to predict

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.210
when your prediction
is gonna stop working.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.820
We have no idea why search
keywords relate to cases

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.210
of the flu you search
based on you, your friends.

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:01.770
Maybe you've heard about Google doing this

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.200
and so you search also
that changes your behavior.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:05.100
We have no idea.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:06.450
And so even just for prediction,

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.700
which has been the thing that
most computer scientists try

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:12.693
to sell you on without causal
relationships we need causes.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.800
Second thing we really
care about is explanation.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.560
I love time series data, so
this is my favorite website.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.570
It pulls lots of time series off the web

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:22.914
and then puts them together.

00:13:22.914 --> 00:13:25.350
So if you have any two
non-stationary time series,

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.330
they're non-stationary in similar ways,

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.310
you can get these really
strong correlations.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.073
A lot of them involve cheese
for some unknown reason.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:34.770
Actually, I gave a talk and showed this

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:36.000
and one person was like, no,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.710
I think this actually is causal,

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.150
they're eating too much cheese

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.400
and then they're, you know, falling asleep

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.780
and then they're actually die suffocating

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.680
because of all the cheese consumption.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.450
And so that was enjoyable.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:49.740
But we can make these stories, right?

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.580
I have another one.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:53.280
Any two exponential time
series look really similar.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.610
Starbucks stores and autism
cases follow the same trend.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.920
I could also say use of cell phones

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:02.280
and autism cases probably
follow a similar trend, right?

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.660
There's nothing causal there,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.000
but because these have
a similar trajectory,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.700
you can make a story but we
don't fully understand them

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.680
unless we understand, oh
these are just, you know,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.180
the cause here is time
and they're changing

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:13.683
in similar ways over time.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.930
Control is probably the most
useful and interesting thing.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.828
Sometimes it's linked with explanation.

00:14:20.828 --> 00:14:24.870
This was a case where used
simulation to understand

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:26.250
causes of singular events.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.483
And so the bold line shows a
person's glucose over time.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.520
The two rises, the
first one is after a run

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.950
and then the later one is after lunch.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:39.780
But we can go back and ask questions,

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.450
what happened if the person
didn't have, didn't go for a run

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.520
or didn't have lunch
or had a smaller lunch?

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:47.190
It's really hard if you
imagine just the bold line

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.960
and you're a person with
diabetes looking at that,

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.810
figuring out why is my glucose
too low right now, right?

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:53.940
To figure out from those two events

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.130
you can't really pick it
apart and say, you know,

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.830
wasn't too much insulin,
wasn't my run was too long

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.960
or too intense, you don't know.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.640
But we can go back with
stipulation and say,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.740
actually in this case it turned out

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.000
it was the two events together.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.980
Each one individually wouldn't have led

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.510
to glucose that was too low.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.550
It was only the combination
of the two events.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.280
And so now once you have that
information in the future,

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.550
you know, this is how I
should change my insulin

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.400
or change my activity or
change my lunch, right,

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.230
to avoid hypoglycemia.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.630
And so you're much more
able to react to the world

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.693
once you have that
information about causes.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.060
So I spent a lot of time arguing

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.110
we need causal Relationships,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:31.980
causes are really important

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.568
if you wanna do anything
useful in the world.

00:15:34.568 --> 00:15:36.510
I wrote a couple books about that

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:39.510
and then the last few years
I've also come to believe

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:43.050
causes are necessary but insufficient.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:44.880
And so the last part of
this talk will be about

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:46.483
that insufficient part

00:15:46.483 --> 00:15:49.233
and why we need understanding
of human behavior.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:52.380
So first I wanted to show a few examples

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.420
of learning from observation.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.860
And so I like to make things difficult.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.710
I like really hard cases
where we can only observe,

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:01.140
I have no ability to
experiment whatsoever.

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.360
I can't even change what gets observed

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.730
and can we find some causal relationships.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:08.370
And so the reason it's interesting is

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.740
I was in bioinformatics as a PhD student

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:14.490
and then EHRs came about and
were becoming much more popular

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:15.870
and I thought, oh this is really cool.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:16.703
We can go back.

00:16:16.703 --> 00:16:18.270
We have 20 years of data on people

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.000
and see what happened
before they became sick.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.920
You learn very quickly that
you don't have 20 years

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.680
of data on everyone and the
number of people is much smaller

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.600
and there's lots of complexity.

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:29.370
But for a person really
interested in time series,

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:31.680
it was very exciting time series data.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.413
I'll talk about a couple
other similar cases.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.680
I won't talk as much
about the technical detail

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.120
but to, you know, have in your head

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.130
what do I mean when I
say causal relationships?

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:43.430
It's not a amazing
network, a graphical model.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.080
My focus is time.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:49.320
I think time is critical for
intervening in the world.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.970
I think we care a lot about the timing

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.830
of the relationships we find.

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:54.540
If you take a medication you really care,

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:55.590
is this going to, you know,

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:58.560
leave my headache in 15
minutes or four hours?

00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.970
If I just tell you it's gonna help you,

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:02.940
you're probably not gonna
be that satisfied with that.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.740
And a lot of relationships
are complicated.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:08.070
You have, you know, combinations
of factors, interactions,

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:11.220
you have one thing happening
until something else happens.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:13.050
And so to me, the natural way

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.760
to represent a causal of relationship is

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.290
with temporal logic.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:20.400
That's what the top thing is
saying that vigorous exercise

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:23.280
leads to hyperglycemia in 15 to 40 minutes

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:25.950
and this happens with probability 0.4.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:26.940
And so that's for me

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.290
what a causal relationship looks like.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:31.620
It has timing, there might be
other stuff on the left side,

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.731
it could be multiple factors,

00:17:33.731 --> 00:17:36.450
maybe the variables are
continuous, that's fine,

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:38.430
they don't have to be discreet

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:40.197
and there's always a time
window associated with that.

00:17:40.197 --> 00:17:42.223
And I also wanna discover
that from the data.

00:17:42.223 --> 00:17:44.894
I assume that I know nothing going in.

00:17:44.894 --> 00:17:47.313
And how do I find causal relationships?

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:49.890
And so basically we're thinking about

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:51.090
what we're doing in the data is

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:52.680
what we'd wanna do if we could experiment,

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:55.380
is imagine toggling the cause on and off

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:57.150
and seeing how the effect changes.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:58.950
And so that's essentially
what we're doing is keeping

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.860
everything else fixed and
then holding the cause,

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:06.120
changing the cause, whether
it's true or false or it's value

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.220
and seeing what happens to the effect.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.570
There's some other details in there,

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:11.010
but that's the basic idea.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.360
And again, there's no Bayesian networks

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.590
and anything that looks
like a Bayesian network

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:15.340
later on is not a Bayesian network.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.860
Just can't be vehement enough about that.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.120
Sometimes people think
they're Bayesian networks

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:23.010
and they get really uncomfortable
because there's cycles.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.980
And so I just wanna make sure
you're not uncomfortable.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:30.150
So the first example is
learning from EHR data.

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.370
So this was a really
poor choice of problem.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:34.890
I decided to work on
congestive heart failure.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.660
And the reason it's a
difficult choice of problem

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.430
or a poor choice of problem is

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.290
that it's really hard from an EHR

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:42.060
to know who has heart failure.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.530
Heart failure is not like a stroke

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:46.140
where you have this acute dramatic event.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.240
You know, in our data we can say roughly

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:51.570
the time the stroke happened,
it doesn't even make sense

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.370
to say when the heart
failure happened, right?

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.070
Heart failure is a chronic
progressive disease.

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:57.900
You have the worsening over time,

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.880
there's not this key onset time

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.860
and then it gets worsened the background

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:03.540
until at some point it's
clinically apparent,

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:04.920
it gets diagnosed.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:06.450
And so really there's like, you know,

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.160
a probability of heart failure over time.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:11.820
And then at some point it
becomes, you know, discernible.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.740
There's not, you know, a
single piece of evidence

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.110
in the record that tells you
if a person has heart failure.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:17.790
You can look at diagnosis codes,

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:20.580
you can look at medications,
you can look at symptoms.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.590
After you do that, you see
there's a lot of people

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.170
who seem to have symptoms
but aren't being treated

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:26.220
or seem to have treatments

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:30.060
but no diagnosis of heart failure
and it becomes very messy.

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:32.190
And so we did that.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.870
We found some ways to figure out

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.270
who had heart failure, who didn't.

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.760
This was data for about 30,000 people

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.863
in rural Pennsylvania, Geisinger.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:42.930
We applied our methods,

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.910
we learned timings from the data.

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:48.330
This is what we find: dx means diagnosis.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:51.000
And again the effect here

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.097
is not actually heart failure, right?

00:19:52.097 --> 00:19:53.580
Because we don't know, we have no idea

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:54.930
when heart failure happens,

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.290
the effect is the
diagnosis of heart failure.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:01.470
And what we're finding is these
other things that are known,

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:04.110
hypothyroidism, diabetes,
being overweight,

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:05.910
they're happening maybe a
year and a half to two years

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.040
before the diagnosis.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.450
Then it becomes really hard to interpret.

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:11.130
You need a lot of background knowledge.

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:14.370
So blood pressure is a
notoriously noisy measurement

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:15.960
in the electronic health record.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.790
We don't find high blood pressure.

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:19.830
We find being on a medication
for high blood pressure

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:22.050
because that's less noisy and
it's a much better indicator

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.990
of whether you have high blood
pressure, the same thing,

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.870
urinary symptoms, that's a
symptom of heart failure,

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:29.312
frequent nighttime urination.

00:20:29.312 --> 00:20:32.220
And that's what we find is
the most common code here.

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.680
And so what happens is
we're finding people,

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.900
it's not that the symptom
is causing heart failure,

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:38.280
that'd be crazy.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.980
The symptom is happening
about 8 to 11 months

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:42.990
before the diagnosis of heart failure.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.240
But it's really hard if someone
just gives you the data,

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.440
you would have no way to interpret this

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:47.910
and know it's happening.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:50.550
We repeated this with data from Columbia,

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:52.380
which was much messier.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:53.760
It was mostly text data.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.980
It's really hard to get
time from text data.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.320
And so we find a lot of
things with medications

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.110
because the medications were
stored in a structured way

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:04.350
and it's much easier to find
the person taking insulin

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:06.780
versus they were diagnosed with diabetes.

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:08.490
And so instead of finding the diabetes

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:09.750
or thyroid problems,

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:11.900
we find the medications
for those diseases.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:17.040
So there were some successes there.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:19.710
I moved on, I thought well, this was hard

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.030
because we don't really
know what's happening

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:22.710
in between the times you see a doctor

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:26.310
but you have, you know, a
couple visits a year maybe

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:28.950
and then what's happening
when you're at home.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:32.310
Chronic diseases are
interesting because one,

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.320
the main things are happening
outside the hospital.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:36.930
If you have diabetes, your
doctor is not there with you

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.720
every time you have a
meal or exercise saying,

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:40.890
here's how much insulin you should take

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.270
to adjust for that, right?

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:43.650
You have to figure that out

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.950
and you're making decisions
all day long about that.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:49.800
Also, the data we have is not, you know,

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.481
research grade sensors, it's
things in the real world

00:21:52.481 --> 00:21:54.783
that become a lot more uncertain.

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:58.530
So just give a sense of that uncertainty.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.547
Key measurement for people
with diabetes is glucose,

00:22:01.547 --> 00:22:04.860
it's a blood glucose which
test with a finger stick

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.050
or now there are sensors
that can measure glucose,

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:10.650
sort of continuously get
measurement every five minutes.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:12.930
So, but they're called continuous.

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:16.530
However, the standards for
these devices are pretty lax.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:20.880
So if you have a value
of 150, maybe it's low,

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.158
maybe it's high, we don't really know.

00:22:23.158 --> 00:22:24.720
Hypoglycemia.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:27.750
So glucose it's too low
can be really dangerous.

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.970
If your glucose is 70, that's pretty norm,

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.130
that's low end of normal.

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:33.570
But we don't know based on the error rates

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:35.820
could be 60, could be 81.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.023
And that's according to the FDA standards.

00:22:38.023 --> 00:22:40.440
Studies have shown that a lot of devices

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.450
don't actually meet those standards.

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:43.860
And so it's a really challenging problem

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:44.880
for causal inference.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.740
If you wanna think how do I find causes

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.780
of hypo or hyperglycemia,

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:50.520
I can't actually trust my measurements.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:53.490
You know, if your value is 69,

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.160
probably it's okay, I don't really know.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:57.078
So I had a small data set

00:22:57.078 --> 00:22:59.250
where we really couldn't
throw away any data

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:00.480
and say we're only going to take

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.760
the values we're really certain about.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.580
So we incorporated uncertainty
into our causal inference.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:09.540
And everything here is
based on it's data-driven.

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:12.660
We're calculating probabilities
in a frequency based manner.

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:14.930
And so I can just wait
my inferences, right?

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:18.060
So I can learn from some values
more strongly than others.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:19.590
And this is what the data looked like.

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:22.020
We had only 17 people over three days.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:24.270
So pretty small data.

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:25.710
There were a lot of different sensors,

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:27.930
they were very active,
they went rock climbing,

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:31.200
they did all kinds of other
stuff in their daily life.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.250
Sensors broke.

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:33.273
That's what happens.

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:35.093
And we wanted to find

00:23:35.093 --> 00:23:37.920
what are the causes of hyper/hypoglycemia.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:39.960
And it was only when we
incorporate the uncertainty

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.340
that we could find anything.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:43.140
If we didn't do that, we basically found

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:45.150
if your glucose is high, it stays high.

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.920
If it's low, it stays low.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:48.870
It's true, but not very interesting

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:50.880
as a causal relationship.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:53.430
But once we incorporated, you
know, what's the probability

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.740
that your glucose is high or low

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.570
given this value that I've measured,

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.610
now we can actually make this finding

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:00.900
that intense activity does lead

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:04.290
to high glucose in 5 to 30 minutes.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:05.280
So it's exciting.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:06.870
But this data set was missing

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:09.690
one of the most important things
for understanding glucose,

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.540
which is meals we didn't
know anything about that.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.060
They were not recorded or
whether they were recorded

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.680
but not timestamped.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:18.360
And so essentially not recorded,

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:21.000
can't use it if you don't know the timing.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.040
One of the challenges and causal inference

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.980
has been often we assume we get data,

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:29.610
we apply our methods to
it, we get some output,

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:30.840
but that means we're starting from scratch

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:32.160
with every data set, right?

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:33.660
Which is not really realistic.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:36.310
We kind of, we hopefully
learn some things over time.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.480
We know some things
about meals and glucose,

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:40.650
it's actually really complicated.

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.120
We don't know everything.

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:45.180
Fat, protein, all these
things affect your glucose

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.283
and interact with carbohydrates.

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:51.300
But we thought we can incorporate
some simple information.

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:53.550
And so here we use knowledge of the effect

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:55.560
of meals on glucose.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.320
We use that to reconstruct these meals.

00:24:58.320 --> 00:24:59.860
We'll talk about the details.

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:03.120
But the key idea is using that
we actually know something

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.760
and then importantly, we
develop ways of figuring out

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:07.290
what our knowledge doesn't apply.

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.000
Because you might learn
something from one experiment

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.550
and it doesn't apply at
all to another population.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:14.220
And there we could actually
find these other relationships.

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:16.920
Now we found, oh, moderate
exercise has a different effect

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:18.333
than intense exercise.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.530
We found that actually
exercise causes people

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:24.630
to have a meal afterwards,
which makes sense.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:25.980
If your glucose is gonna be low

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:27.510
after moderate exercise,

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.220
you're gonna compensate with a meal.

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:31.710
And then other methods
didn't find anything.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:34.170
I could have a whole separate
talk about other methods

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:35.760
that didn't work at all on real data,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.110
but we won't do that today.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.580
And so the key has been it's really hard

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:44.045
to find causal
relationships in these data,

00:25:44.045 --> 00:25:46.953
but it's possible with some creativity.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.340
Third application I wanted to mention

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:51.173
is looking at stroke data.

00:25:51.173 --> 00:25:54.000
So this is really different
in terms of frequency compared

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:55.500
to the other data sets.

00:25:55.500 --> 00:26:00.270
So in the hospital data,
data for 30,000 people

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:02.040
is about the same size as data

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:03.450
for 100 stroke patients,

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:05.340
just if you're talking about this size.

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:08.370
So these are about 100 really
intensively monitored people.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:11.790
This is the situation
you saw in the picture

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:13.230
of the ICU patient.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:15.750
Those sensors are measuring
every one to five seconds

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.420
the entire time someone's in the ICU.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.240
The measurements depend on the
patient's status of course,

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:23.730
and I have no ability to change that.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.740
We have EEG on the surface of the brain,

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.410
EEG within the brain, microdialysis,

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:31.890
physiologic measurements,
but a lot's missing.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:35.880
And so this is three
patients, X axis is time.

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:37.740
And the Y axis are different variables,

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:40.710
which are different for
the different patients.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:44.520
The top person you can see
as during their ICU stay,

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:46.530
there's just more
variables over time, right?

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:48.720
As their status changes, the
doctors think other things

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:51.150
are important and maybe
we should measure 'em.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:52.620
The middle person

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:56.280
this is a variable that's
measured every hour

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:57.900
by a human writing down the value.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:00.240
And you can see these
aren't evenly spaced, right?

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:02.970
Because it's an ICU, it's
a complex environment,

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:05.430
lots of things are happening
and it's not anyone's priority

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:06.510
to write down this number

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.610
exactly every hour on the hour, right?

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:10.440
It's not gonna happen.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.990
Last one, there's huge chunks of time

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:15.930
with all variables missing
because maybe there's a surgery

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.450
or procedure, everything's
disconnected for that.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:21.667
Other times things just
slip out of where they were.

00:27:21.667 --> 00:27:24.570
And so if you think of this
in terms of causal inference,

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:26.880
none of this missing data
is missing at random, right?

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:28.980
It's all missing for a reason,

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:31.200
which is generally the
patient's health status

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:32.940
or things that are
happening to the patient

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:34.440
or about the patient.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.670
And so if we ignore that,

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:38.370
then we have a huge problem
with confounding, right?

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:41.460
And so we spent five years dealing

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:43.050
with missing data problems.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.970
How do we reconstruct missing values

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:48.270
when you have all variables
missing at a time point,

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:51.660
when the relationships are
lagged between the variables.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:54.183
We developed methods
for all of those things.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.510
So it was, we started working
with this data in 2010.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:04.430
By 2015 we actually got a causal
model that we believed in.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:08.970
So this is part of the model
and again, not a busy network.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.610
So the cycles are fine.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.620
I just don't know how to
represent temporal relationships

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:14.670
in a satisfying way.

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:16.200
And so there's no time on the graph,

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:17.730
but there's time everywhere.

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:20.370
And some of these relationships
could be positive,

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:21.540
some could be negative.

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:23.140
I'm not showing you any of that.

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:27.120
And the doctors thought there
was this important transition

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:28.200
at 96 hours.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:30.210
So we just crudely separated the data

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:33.780
in these two time periods,
did our causal inference

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:34.740
for the first period

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:37.710
and then for the second
period we compared them.

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:39.000
And the things to note is one,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.100
there's just more cause
relationships over time,

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:42.540
which was interesting.

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:45.120
And the thing that
doctors cared about a lot

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:47.160
was this TW percent to the bottom,

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:48.990
which represents brain swelling.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.210
And so if your causes
are changing over time,

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:53.760
it starts off with brain
temperature later on.

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:57.090
It's brain oxygenation,
blood flow in the brain,

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:58.020
maybe your interventions also

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.100
have to be different over time, right?

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.200
If your causes change over time.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:02.490
So that was super exciting.

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:04.860
Oh, and the numbers are
relate to the number of people

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:06.090
that relationship was found in

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:09.000
because people have
different things measured.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.970
However, this is what the
entire model looked like.

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.710
For me, this was five years of work.

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:15.600
I was so excited after this.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.880
This was like we actually did it.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.340
We've solved stroke obviously

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:22.230
and gave this to the doctor,
you're gonna use this

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:23.730
to treat your patients now, right?

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:25.350
I've solved all of your problems.

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:27.153
And he called this unholy mess.

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:29.430
He's German and very blunt.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:33.270
And I thought, okay, that's not exactly

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:34.620
what I was hoping to hear.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:36.330
We spent like three
hours going through this.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:38.700
He had a lot of questions,
why is this not there?

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:40.470
Well it had no variation.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:42.870
If there's no variation, we
can't find causal relationships

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:45.138
and, you know, why is this there?

00:29:45.138 --> 00:29:48.240
Why is that that not there?

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:49.980
And then his questions were, okay,

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:51.540
if heart rate causes this,

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:52.523
what happens if I give this medication?

00:29:52.523 --> 00:29:54.000
I said, I have no idea.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:55.560
Medication's on a variable in the graph

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:58.860
and so I can't tell you
anything about it, right?

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:01.537
Which is the key problem
is everything here is,

00:30:01.537 --> 00:30:04.770
you know, the variables we
measure, his questions are

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.630
what ifs about interventions, right?

00:30:06.630 --> 00:30:08.400
And none of the interventions
are on a variable

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.380
and that like do calculus, you know,

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:12.300
turning a variable on and off.

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:13.680
They're all, I have a strategy

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.360
where I can maybe change temperature

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:17.640
or, you know, change blood flow,

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.470
but I don't have direct
access to that variable

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.630
and I couldn't help him at all.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:26.430
So then after five years, hopefully, yeah,

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:29.070
this was my feeling about that

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:30.720
was maybe I'm the problem, right?

00:30:32.089 --> 00:30:33.510
I've been telling everyone,

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:34.770
causal relationships are super important.

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:36.840
We need causal relationships
to do anything.

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:39.270
If you're gonna make
decisions or interventions

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:41.160
or predictions, you need causes.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.250
And then it turns out my
collaborator, who, you know,

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:45.930
literally has his fingers
in people's brains

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:47.230
couldn't use these models.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:51.000
So starting in about 2017, I wondered,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.300
does this actually help
people make decisions?

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:57.120
And if we look at how we
evaluate our causal models,

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.040
we don't evaluate
decision making of course,

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.710
or computer scientists,
we evaluate accuracy

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:03.206
and more is better pretty much.

00:31:03.206 --> 00:31:05.730
There's different, you
know, different metrics,

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:06.930
but they're all kind of the same thing

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.060
and they have the same shared values.

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:10.560
And the value is did you find

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:12.000
more of the causes that are there?

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:14.430
And did you find fewer wrong causes?

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:15.630
And maybe sometimes we care about

00:31:15.630 --> 00:31:19.080
how fast your algorithm runs
or how much data it needs,

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:20.310
but those are kind of secondary.

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:21.960
It's really more is better

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:25.309
finding the most complete
model you can possibly find.

00:31:25.309 --> 00:31:26.490
And so I thought, okay,

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:28.890
psychologists knows some
things about people.

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:30.720
They've done a lot of work on causality.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:32.520
This is kind of what it looks like.

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:33.990
Reminds me a little bit
of the Bayesian networks

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:35.553
that I found as a PhD student.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:38.640
There are these very
constrained setups, right?

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:39.990
They're trying to do controlled studies

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:43.710
and figure out what can
people do interventions

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:46.050
for causal relationships,
in what cases, right?

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.760
When can they infer causes?

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:49.620
And so there are all these book detectors

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:53.114
where you put blocks on a
box and see if it makes music

00:31:53.114 --> 00:31:56.160
or there's like made up microorganisms.

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:58.830
My favorite is mind reading aliens

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:00.900
probably you have no experience
with mind reading aliens.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:01.860
So you have to rely totally

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:03.570
on what the researchers tell you, right?

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:06.780
You're not combining this at
all with your past knowledge,

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.340
which is really different
from what happened

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:09.630
with my collaborator, right?

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:11.220
He's not looking just at what I told him.

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.260
He's thinking, yeah, but
I have this hypothesis

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:14.880
about this relationship,
why isn't that there?

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:15.720
Or what is this other one?

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:17.400
How does that fit into what I know?

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.620
And that's what happens when
we get guidance about health

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:21.300
and finance and all these
other things, right?

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:23.100
We're not just taking, you know,

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.100
this information about
a vaccine in a vacuum.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:27.750
We're fitting it in with our prior beliefs

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:29.880
about the disease, vaccines in general,

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.500
all this other stuff.

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:34.230
So I started doing decision making studies

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:36.150
where people made everyday choices

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:39.810
and they were a little bit more realistic.

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:41.853
So this was the first one that we did.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:45.420
We had a student, they have,

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:46.470
they just started college.

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:48.320
They're adjusting to a busy schedule.

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:50.610
She's trying to avoid gaining weight

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:52.140
but also wants to make friends

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.720
and leave time for homework and studying.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.880
And so we showed people causal model

00:32:56.880 --> 00:33:00.510
or the same model as just
text or no causal model.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:02.608
And at this point I was still optimistic.

00:33:02.608 --> 00:33:04.950
I thought this is like
taking an open book exam.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:06.570
If I give you the causal model,

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:08.880
even if you already know
the information, you know,

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:12.690
it's like it's reminding you
right of what you already know.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.130
And that's not at all what happened.

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:17.160
And so we found causal
models made things worse.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.260
People consistently made worse choices.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:22.320
And it didn't matter if
it was a diagram or text,

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.872
people made worse choices,
by the causal model.

00:33:24.872 --> 00:33:26.430
So that was really disappointing

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:29.700
after all my years spent
trying to find causal models

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:31.350
from really, really difficult data

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:33.000
and also convincing
people that causal models

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.070
were really important and useful.

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:36.330
And we looked at everything.

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:37.590
We looked at, you know,

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:39.240
how much time do people spend on it?

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:40.380
Were they ignoring it?

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:41.880
No, they spent longer with it.

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:46.440
We did again, maybe about
four years of interventions

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:49.290
of what if we show people
how little they know.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:50.460
If we give them like quizzes

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.290
or help them reflect on their knowledge,

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:54.090
maybe they'll be more
open to new information

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:55.980
because they'll realize they
actually need the information.

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:56.880
It didn't work at all.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:58.860
People knew that they
didn't know anything.

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.620
They tried to use the
model and they couldn't.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:02.670
And in all these cases,

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:04.260
people were also doing these questions

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.630
about aliens and robots
and things like that.

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:08.430
And they had no problem
in those cases, right?

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:10.830
They were totally fine when
the question was about robots.

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:13.440
But once it was about, you
know, saving for retirement

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:15.210
or managing their diabetes,

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.850
suddenly they were doing
terribly on these questions

00:34:17.850 --> 00:34:19.620
and also they were losing
confidence as well.

00:34:19.620 --> 00:34:22.320
And so we measured how they
felt about their decisions

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:24.770
and they felt worse about their
choices with a causal model.

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:26.929
So it was really quite that.

00:34:26.929 --> 00:34:29.880
And so my mental model of how
things were gonna go was this,

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:31.440
at the start I was like, you know,

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.390
you have this gap in your knowledge.

00:34:33.390 --> 00:34:34.920
I'm coming in with my causal model,

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:36.720
I'm going to slot it right into that gap.

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:38.913
And, you know, problem solved.

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:43.320
And I think it was more
like this, that, you know,

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:44.820
you already have all these beliefs,

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:46.140
I'm giving you information,

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:48.480
it does not fit cleanly
into these other things

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.820
and I haven't helped you
figure out how to map it

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:52.113
to what you already know.

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:56.190
And so skipping ahead a few years,

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:58.020
finally we have some positive results.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:34:59.364
So we'll end on a good note.

00:34:59.364 --> 00:35:02.640
What if we focus people's attention?

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:03.930
And so it wasn't people's willingness

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:05.100
to use the causal model.

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:06.510
It wasn't that they didn't understand

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:07.650
what a causal model means.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:09.450
They understood that perfectly well.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:12.360
We think that it was that
they didn't really know

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:14.670
which parts of it to
use or how to combine it

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.257
with their existing knowledge.

00:35:16.257 --> 00:35:19.920
And so we started doing
studies testing that.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:23.190
So using either very complex model

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:25.470
or giving people just the
parts that were relevant.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:27.450
And so in this case, if you think back

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:29.400
to the diagram I showed you before,

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.710
you could use that for lots
of different questions.

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:32.900
And that's what we often
try to create, right?

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.560
As a general model, you could use it

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:36.240
for many different purposes.

00:35:36.240 --> 00:35:38.790
We don't specifically tailor
it to this is one decision

00:35:38.790 --> 00:35:39.750
that you're gonna make,

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:41.800
here's the right model for that decision.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:43.767
But what if we did right?

00:35:43.767 --> 00:35:46.140
And so here we're showing
people different parts

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:47.670
of the model depending on the question

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:48.990
that they're answering.

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:51.870
And so that's why there, there
are different models here

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:53.280
and some of them have cycles.

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:58.200
Here the dashed edges are
negative causal relationships

00:35:58.200 --> 00:36:01.920
because it was based on a
well-known diagram about obesity

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.110
that used that similar format.

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:05.190
That was a very complex model.

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:06.630
But I won't show you that one.

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:08.220
Questions looked very similar.

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:11.253
Again, we have a person,
they have some constraints.

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:14.730
This one, they don't have that much time.

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:16.320
They're concerned about their weight

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:17.490
and they have a few options.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.220
And again, the options
aren't variables, right?

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.740
It's not, you know, increase carbohydrates

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:23.820
or increase exercise.

00:36:23.820 --> 00:36:26.010
It's what exact strategy
you're going to do,

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.680
which is much harder and much,
much more different, right?

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:29.880
So he could do nothing.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:31.710
He could fast forward through commercials,

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:35.400
he could get takeout or change
up his recipes a little bit.

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:38.220
So finally we had some positive results.

00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:40.260
Turns out there was no
difference between the simple

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:44.340
and the complex diagram
and no information,

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:46.440
but simplicity that that
turned out to be the answer.

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:48.540
Giving people just the
information they needed,

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:51.603
putting blinders on them
led to better results.

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:56.130
The interesting thing
is you can also use that

00:36:56.130 --> 00:37:00.900
to change people's preferences
in maybe undesirable ways.

00:37:00.900 --> 00:37:03.240
And so it's known that
people prefer intervening

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:07.560
on root notes compared to
internal or direct causes.

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:09.810
And so we tested that
with this causal chain.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:13.470
Seems a bit, you know,
more sensible or if you're,

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:16.530
you have a probabilistic model,
it's mathematically superior

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:19.110
to intervene on the direct cause, right?

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:22.380
So you'd think why not
directly change someone's diet

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:25.380
versus like not watch as
much TV so you're not exposed

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:27.450
to food ads and then
have an unhealthy diet

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:28.380
because of the food ads.

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:32.100
That's sort of indirect and
could fail at different points.

00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:33.570
But when we show people this model,

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.930
then in this simple model,
they picked the food ads more.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:40.680
And so you do have to be careful
how you simplify the model.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:41.760
But we expanded beyond health.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:43.553
We tried lots of
different decision making.

00:37:43.553 --> 00:37:47.790
So picking a college, lowering
your carbon footprint,

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:50.280
mental health, all these different cases.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:51.330
And the exciting news was

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.910
we found the exact same
thing in every domain.

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:56.040
Simple models helped.

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:59.130
We showed if you just
highlight the relevant path,

00:37:59.130 --> 00:38:02.280
so make an orange, draw
attention to it, that works.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:04.890
So you don't have to just, you
know, hide the whole model,

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.310
but you have to show people
what part of the model is.

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:09.240
So I started wondering, you know,

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:11.610
is there some level of
complexity that's right?

00:38:11.610 --> 00:38:12.443
You know, maybe there's something

00:38:12.443 --> 00:38:14.160
in between these like really big models

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.890
and really small one and tested.

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:19.230
What if we just add in
a little bit of extra,

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:21.960
couple extra nodes, couple extra edges.

00:38:21.960 --> 00:38:24.303
No, that was not helpful at all.

00:38:25.191 --> 00:38:27.030
And the results were basically the same

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:29.340
as if you give people a
completely complicated model.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:31.740
And so any extra information
gives you an opportunity

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:33.093
to overthink basically.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:35.010
And so if you think of like

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:37.860
when you give your students
exams and they have questions

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:39.540
during the test about a question

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.120
that you think was maybe very easy,

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:43.020
they're overthinking, right?

00:38:43.020 --> 00:38:46.710
I have these questions, I
gave an exam last spring

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.450
and the easiest possible question

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:51.570
people had the most questions
about for me during the exam.

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:52.620
And they're like, well what about this?

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:55.050
Or should I, you know, like
rely on my knowledge of calculus

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:56.129
and all that?

00:38:56.129 --> 00:38:57.120
And like, I'm like, no, it really is.

00:38:57.120 --> 00:38:58.200
Just take it at face value.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.843
It's as simple as you think it is.

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:02.520
And, you know, as you know more,

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:04.980
you have more opportunity
to overthink, right?

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:07.500
And so leaving any possibility
for that, people start.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:09.390
And we've done other studies
where people describe

00:39:09.390 --> 00:39:11.610
their decision making
process and they add in

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:13.440
so much extra stuff
that's not in the model.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:15.270
They talk about, you know,

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:16.680
their preferences about medications.

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:18.120
It's really hard to take medications.

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:19.920
Maybe they should start
with this, you know,

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:21.720
if they develop a routine
and they get a dog

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:23.730
and they walk the dog,
then this'll happen.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:26.940
And then that'll solve
their blood pressure, right?

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:28.740
And so that's why you would
don't have the opportunity

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:29.573
to do that

00:39:29.573 --> 00:39:31.290
when the questions about
aliens and robots, right?

00:39:31.290 --> 00:39:32.910
There's no potential for like coming up

00:39:32.910 --> 00:39:35.430
with these really convoluted stories.

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.650
Still, you might think like, you know,

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:40.140
you have a PhD probably you know yourself,

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:41.910
you like getting a lot of information,

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:43.260
you would do better than
people in the studies,

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:46.470
if I just let you choose how
much information you want.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:47.670
And so we tested that, right?

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:49.800
Instead of randomizing
people, let them choose

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:51.210
and let them choose for each question.

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:52.620
And so they would see here's

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:54.660
what all these types of
information look like

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:56.400
and your next question's going

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:58.770
to be about retirement or finances.

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:00.420
How much information do you want?

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:05.430
So a good fraction of people
pick the simple diagram,

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:06.480
which is the best choice.

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:08.430
So that was good news.

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:10.290
However, people don't know
how much information they need

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:11.460
and people who think they need

00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:14.970
the most complicated information
or no information at all

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:16.080
are not right.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:18.240
Those numbers are almost exactly the same

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:19.640
as when we randomized people

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:22.560
and simple diagrams, again,

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:24.570
were still had the highest accuracy.

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:27.900
And then we had other data that was fun.

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:29.340
It turned out that women did better

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:31.830
in all information conditions than men.

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:33.780
Also people with less
education did better.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:35.580
Again, with the overthinking,

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:37.050
the more you know, the
more opportunity you have

00:40:37.050 --> 00:40:39.210
to like come up with
all these extra things,

00:40:39.210 --> 00:40:40.950
all these extra ideas add information

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:43.860
that's just not relevant
and not useful at all

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:45.333
to the diagram.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:49.170
But again, we were
excited that after, again,

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:51.570
about five years, finally
we made causal models

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:54.150
better than nothing, which
was really depressing

00:40:54.150 --> 00:40:57.870
for giving talks was just,
and for a while it was,

00:40:57.870 --> 00:40:58.950
we can make causal models

00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:01.053
as good as nothing but not better.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:03.573
So this was a nice improvement.

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:06.630
But going back to the beginning,

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:08.730
so I've changed my tune a little bit

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:11.070
that completeness is not the right thing

00:41:11.070 --> 00:41:12.720
to value in causal models, right?

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:15.330
It might be helpful for, you
know, scientific accuracy

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:17.580
and understanding, but if we really care

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:21.090
about decision making or
interventions, that's not,

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:22.200
that's not what we need, right?

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.450
And so we need new ways of
thinking about what is utility

00:41:24.450 --> 00:41:26.430
for a causal model.

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:29.340
Here, we didn't take into
account people's decision context

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:32.580
how much time they have,
what their background is.

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:33.413
We didn't do any of that.

00:41:33.413 --> 00:41:36.153
And so there's a lot of other
stuff to be explored there.

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:42.050
Also, other things I didn't
mention fund dirty laundry.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.100
I didn't tell you how I find variables.

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:45.360
I don't know what a variable is.

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:46.440
I have no idea how to tell you

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:48.371
what the right variables
are for your problem.

00:41:48.371 --> 00:41:50.616
But everything, they're really important

00:41:50.616 --> 00:41:52.080
and all guarantees are relative

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.580
to having the right variables
and so get it right.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:58.607
Our assumptions are almost
never true, you know?

00:41:58.607 --> 00:42:00.930
And so we can't guarantee
that anything is causal

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:03.990
because all the assumptions
we need to make are false.

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:06.180
I also have no idea how much data I need.

00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:08.130
You know, there's no
power analysis whatsoever.

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:10.830
More is probably better,
but we don't know.

00:42:10.830 --> 00:42:12.300
It's, you know, I can sometimes tell you

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:14.760
after the fact that I
didn't have enough data,

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:16.590
but I can't tell you proactively
here's how much you need

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:18.450
to collect to find a causal model.

00:42:18.450 --> 00:42:22.770
And so there's huge gaps in
finding causal models from data,

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:25.500
even though I've shown
you some success stories.

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:28.110
So, and there and just think

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:29.190
all the different collaborators

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:30.720
that were involved in this work.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:31.703
Thanks.

00:42:31.703 --> 00:42:34.870
(audience applauding)

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:40.815
- Thank you so much for giving
us the unvarnished truth

00:42:40.815 --> 00:42:42.870
about causal modeling.

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:45.450
So I just want to invite
anyone who wants to speak

00:42:45.450 --> 00:42:48.000
to just press the button on the microphone

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.793
and speak up.

00:42:52.541 --> 00:42:54.630
I'm happy to start with
the first question.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:59.630
So I was really curious, how are you,

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:00.840
so in this,

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.410
in the process of making
those causal change,

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:07.110
are you marginalizing
over prior information

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:08.380
that people have

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:13.230
or is prior information
is that really not part

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:15.472
of the whole scheme?

00:43:15.472 --> 00:43:18.450
- We haven't incorporated like
individual's prior knowledge.

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:19.283
We don't know.

00:43:19.283 --> 00:43:20.116
We've done other studies trying

00:43:20.116 --> 00:43:22.980
to just quantify someone's
degree of knowledge

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:25.980
about a problem, but haven't used that.

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:27.240
We haven't made predictions, for example,

00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:29.850
about like what would someone choose.

00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:31.710
We've done other work looking at

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:33.780
where we got people's mental models.

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:34.650
Another fun project is

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:37.680
how do you know you got someone's
mental model accurately?

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:38.880
How do you evaluate mental models?

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.740
That's been been fun.

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:41.790
But mostly what we found there was

00:43:41.790 --> 00:43:43.230
we couldn't change people's mental models.

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:46.479
They had the same mental model
before and before and after.

00:43:46.479 --> 00:43:49.890
Yeah.

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:52.743
- David, then Sean and then Eduardo.

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:55.410
- Just very briefly, that
was really fascinating.

00:43:55.410 --> 00:43:57.314
I have lots of questions.

00:43:57.314 --> 00:44:01.110
For the last main part of the presentation

00:44:01.110 --> 00:44:04.620
where simple models were
better all around, right?

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:07.110
That's what I took at
least the message to be.

00:44:07.110 --> 00:44:08.260
Can you say a bit more?

00:44:09.312 --> 00:44:12.960
If I follow correctly,
your team was trying to,

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:13.800
at least in some of these instances,

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:16.530
trying to build a more complete model

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:17.970
and then work from there to simplify

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:19.528
or am I missing, am I forgetting?

00:44:19.528 --> 00:44:21.213
- Well, we're working to,

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:23.340
on all the health data,

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:27.060
the goal was find all the causal
relationships in the data.

